The rules saying what the resource may exchange network packets with, and
what it may not, in both directions. One rule per line: `allow` or `deny`,
a noun, then what qualifies the noun:

    net_rules =
        allow node n2
        deny network backend node n3
        allow network backend
        allow object ./svc/db
        allow object ./svc/{name}-db rid ip#1
        allow namespace .
        deny address 192.168.10.5
        allow address 192.168.10.0/24
        allow address fd00:10::5
        allow address from ./cfg/acl key allowed
        allow object ./svc/db port 5432 proto tcp

The rules are an ordered list, and the first one matching a packet decides
of it, from the top: a packet from or to an address an `allow` rule names
passes, one from or to an address a `deny` rule names is dropped, and the
rules after the one that matched are not read. So an exception is written
before the rule it is carved out of: `deny network backend node n3` before
`allow network backend` allows the network but the part of the node n3, and
written after it would decide of nothing.

Every list ends with three rules nobody writes, in this order:

    allow localhost
    allow dns
    deny any

What no rule matches is denied: a list says what is let through, and a list
with no `allow` rule lets through the loopback addresses and the nameservers
alone, which the validation warns of. A last `allow any` turns it around:
the list then says what is denied, and the rest passes.

`localhost` and `dns` are allowed without a rule saying so: a filter written
for the peers of an application is not meant to cut it from its own node,
nor from the names it resolves. They come last, just before the `deny any`,
so a written rule matching them wins: `deny localhost` or `deny dns` takes
them away, and so does a `deny` of what holds them, as `deny cluster` where
the nameservers are the ones of `cluster.dns`, addresses of the cluster
nodes, unless `allow dns` is written before it. The node itself is reached
over the loopback addresses only: its other addresses, as the one its om
listener is on, need a `node` or `cluster` rule. The om socket, a file, is
not filtered. `deny any` alone cuts the resource from the network, its own
node included.

No rule, the keyword unset or empty, is no filter at all.

A service with an address on the `lan` network, reaching the objects of its
namespace, the nameservers and the world, and nothing else of the cluster,
its nodes and its networks:

    net_rules =
        allow namespace .
        allow dns
        deny cluster
        deny network lan
        allow any

The nouns:

* `localhost`: the loopback addresses, `127.0.0.0/8` and `::1`.
* `dns`: the nameservers what the resource runs resolves through, see
  below.
* `any`: every address.
* `address <address or range>`: an ipv4 or ipv6 address, or a range.
* `address from <datastore> key <key>`: the addresses and ranges a key of a
  cfg or a sec holds, one per line, as `./cfg/acl` or `./sec/acl`, of the
  namespace of this object or of a datastore another namespace shares with
  it, see below.
* `network <name>`: the range of a cluster network. Followed by `node
  <nodename>`, the part of it that node hands out.
* `node <nodename>`: a cluster node, the address of its listener and the
  ones its name resolves to.
* `cluster`: every cluster node.
* `object <path>`: the addresses the resources of an object publish in
  their status, as its ip resources, the instances of all the nodes
  together. `./svc/db` is the object of the namespace of this one,
  `prod/svc/db` one of another namespace, and `{name}` and `{namespace}`
  are expanded. Followed by `rid <resource id>`, the addresses of that
  resource alone.
* `namespace <name>`: the addresses of every object of a namespace, `.`
  being the one of this object.

Any rule may end with `port <port> proto tcp|udp`, or `port <first>-<last>
proto tcp|udp`, on a kvm container, see below: it then names that port of
what it names, and no other.

A value written on one line holds several rules, as a command sets it: a
verb starts a rule where the one before it is complete, so `allow network
backend allow node n2` is two rules, and `allow network backend node n2`
one, the part of the network the node hands out. A rule has one verb and
one noun: `allow node n1 node n2` is refused, the verb being written again
for each.

    om <path> config update --set 'container#1.net_rules=allow namespace . deny cluster allow any'

A rule added with `net_rules+=` is added after the others, so after a last
`allow any`, where it decides of nothing: the validation warns of it. The
order being what the rules mean, a list is changed by writing its value
again, whole, or by editing the configuration.

The validation warns of a rule that can never match, a rule before it
matching all it names whatever the cluster holds: any rule after an `allow
any` or a `deny any`, a `node` after `cluster`, an `object` after its
`namespace`, a network narrowed to a node after the network, an address
after a range holding it, a rule written twice. It judges the rules as
written, and resolves nothing: two rules resolving to the same addresses on
this cluster are not warned of. These are warnings: the configuration is
committed, and the filter is the one of the rules as written.

`dns`, written or allowed without a rule, is the nameservers of the
resolver of what is filtered, and no other:

* An app, and a task running a command on the node: the nameservers the
  `/etc/resolv.conf` of the node names. Where it names a local stub, as the
  `127.0.0.53` of systemd-resolved, the command talks to the stub alone,
  and the servers the stub forwards to, the ones of `cluster.dns` among
  them, are not opened.
* A docker or podman container, and a task running one: the nameservers om
  writes in the `/etc/resolv.conf` of the container, which are the ones of
  the `dns` keyword of the networks its addresses are on, else the ones of
  `cluster.dns`, then the ones its `dns` keyword adds, the first three, as
  many as a resolver reads. A container om writes no resolver for, no
  nameserver being named, keeps the one of its engine: the nameservers the
  `/etc/resolv.conf` of the node names are allowed.
* A lxc container: the nameservers the `/etc/resolv.conf` of the node
  names, om writing no resolver for it. A container resolving through
  others, as the ones its own `/etc/resolv.conf` names, needs a rule naming
  them.
* A kvm container: the nameservers of the `dns` keyword of the networks its
  `network` keyword names, which om tells the guest. A guest told none
  resolves through what it is configured with, which om does not know: no
  nameserver is allowed without a rule naming it.

A nameserver allowed is reached on every port. The nameservers of
`cluster.dns` are addresses of the cluster nodes, so a container resolving
through them reaches whatever these nodes serve. Naming
in the `dns` keyword of a network an address dedicated to the dns on that
network opens that address alone to the containers of the network. An
address that is also one of a node, where services are bound to every
address, still reaches them.

A rule naming a network, a node, an object, a resource or a namespace the
cluster does not have, or an object not started yet, resolves to nothing,
and matches nothing until what it names exists: an `allow` rule lets
nothing more through, and a `deny` rule denies nothing, leaving the packets
to the rules after it. The validation of the
configuration warns of what does not exist, naming the object as resolved,
`./svc/db` being `test/svc/db` in the `test` namespace. It says nothing of
the objects of another namespace: the logs of the start do.

The rules are resolved when the resource starts: the networks and the nodes
from the configuration of the node, the objects from the daemon. With the
daemon down, the resource starts all the same, its `object` and `namespace`
rules resolving to nothing, with a warning, until the daemon is back.

The daemon then keeps the filter of the running resource current. When an
object named starts, stops, moves or changes address, when an object joins
or leaves a namespace named, when a node joins or a network changes, it
resolves the rules again and sets the filter, which applies at once to what
the resource runs, without a restart. An address every instance of an
object publishes, as a failover address, is the same wherever the object
runs: its failover changes nothing. A daemon starting sets the filters
again.

The filter fails closed, on the side letting less through when the daemon
does not know:

* An address of an `allow` rule is taken away when the status of its
  object no longer publishes it, or the object is deleted. An address drawn from a network
  is released when its resource stops, where an address the configuration
  names is published started or not. It is kept while that status is not
  known, the daemon of a node of the object being down or restarting, which
  stops nothing of what the object runs.
  The delete of the object takes it away at once, a node of the object
  being down or not: a node back without the object says nothing of it.
* An address of a `deny` rule stays denied when its object stops
  publishing it, as what answers there may still be the object, until the
  object is deleted.
* Either is forgotten as soon as another object publishes the address.

An address kept is kept by the rule that resolved to it, at the place of
that rule in the list and on its ports: an object named by an `allow` rule
and by a `deny` rule has its addresses followed by each, and one a `deny`
rule keeps is still denied before the rules written after it.

A change of the rules themselves, of `net_rules`, their order included, is
applied by the daemon to the resource as it runs, on the node running it, within
seconds and without a restart: the one change of the configuration it
applies with no action asked, a filter being a security setting. Rules
tightened, loosened or removed are set as the configuration is written, and
a daemon starting applies the ones changed while it was down. Rules the
validation refuses change nothing: the filter in force is kept, and the
status of the resource warns until they are fixed.

Rules given to a resource running with none are set under it when what it
runs is in a systemd unit of its slice: a container, and an app started
with `systemd_properties`. The start command of an app started with neither
rules nor `systemd_properties` runs in no unit, where nothing is filtered:
the rules apply at its next start, and its status warns until then. So do
the rules given to a container its start would have been refused with, as
one whose engine places it with the cgroupfs driver, and to a lxc container
started by an agent that made no scope of it, before its upgrade. A run of a task in
progress is given the rules as a running app or container is.

The status of the resource shows the filter in force: what each rule
resolves to, in the order of the rules, the implicit ones included, up to
the rule matching every address, which ends the list. The addresses of the
rules naming another namespace are counted there, not listed.

The key of an `address from <datastore> key <key>` rule holds one address
or range per line, ipv4 or ipv6. An empty line, and a line starting with
`#`, is skipped. A line that is neither is left out, the others applying,
and warned of by the validation and by the start, by its number, and by its
text for a cfg. The datastore is one of the namespace of the object, `./`
or the namespace spelled out, or one of another namespace sharing its keys
with it, as `infra/cfg/acl` with `share = <namespace>` or `share = *`, the
keys a resource installs being read the same way: the `share` keyword is
the consent of who administers the datastore, and no grant on its namespace
is asked of who writes the rule. A rule naming a datastore of another
namespace that does not share its keys is an error of the validation, for
root too, and resolves to nothing, in an `allow` rule and in a `deny` rule
alike.
A datastore or a key that does not exist resolves to nothing too, with a
warning, until it does.

The key is read when the resource starts, on its node, and the daemon keeps
the filter current: a key added, changed or removed, as by `om <datastore>
key change`, changes the configuration of the datastore, and the daemon of
the node resolves the rules reading it again when it has it, and sets the
filter under the running resource, with no restart. A line removed is an
address taken away at once. The `share` keyword is read with the key, each
time: a datastore that stops sharing its keys with the namespace of the
object has its addresses taken out of the running filters, the daemon
warning of it, as if its key was removed.

The key of a sec is read by om, as root on the node, as the keys a resource
installs are. Its addresses are in the filter, and not in the status of the
resource, which counts them as it counts the ones of the rules naming
another namespace: who reads the status of a resource does not read the
keys of a sec. The addresses of a cfg are shown, its keys being read by who
reads its namespace, and the ones of a cfg of another namespace too, its
`share` keyword having given them to the namespace of the object. The logs
of the daemon, root's, show the whole filter.

The filter of an app, a task and a docker, podman or lxc container knows
no port: a rule with a `port` qualifier is refused there, by the validation
and at the start, and only a kvm container takes it.

A port is the one of what the rule names, not one of the guest: `allow
object ./svc/db port 5432 proto tcp` lets the guest talk to the port 5432 of
the addresses of the object, from any port of its own, and `deny any port
25 proto tcp`, written first, keeps it from every smtp server, the ones the
rules after it open whole included. The filter follows no
connection: a packet sent to the address passes by its destination port,
and one received from it by its source port. A guest serving a port to
clients is opened to them by a rule with no port. The other protocols of an
address opened on a port only, as the icmp of a ping, do not pass. The
first rule matching decides there too: a rule on a port matches the packets
of that port alone, and leaves the others to the rules after it.

The filter of an app, a task and a docker, podman or lxc container is the
`IPAddressAllow` and `IPAddressDeny` of the systemd slice of the process
group of the resource, enforced by the kernel on its cgroup, whatever
network namespace its processes are in. systemd knows no order, an allowed
range winning over a denied one: it is given the two lists letting through
exactly what the ordered rules do, each `allow` rule less what the `deny`
rules before it name, so `systemctl show` tells ranges carved around the
exceptions, where the status of the resource tells the rules in their
order. A list may be thousands of ranges, as a wide range allowed after
tens of ipv6 addresses denied one by one inside it, each carving it in up
to 128 ranges: a last `allow any` carves nothing, the addresses denied
before it being the denied list as they are. A filter systemd does not
take, as when it can not be reached, is an error: the start fails, and a
running resource keeps the filter in force, the daemon logging the error.
It needs systemd, the
unified cgroup hierarchy and a kernel attaching BPF programs to the cgroups:
the resource refuses to start where one is missing, rather than run
unfiltered, and so does a rootless container, whose group is delegated to
the systemd instance of a user. The `IPAddressAllow` and `IPAddressDeny` of
`systemd_properties` are still taken, and a packet passes when both let it.

What is filtered:

* An app, and a task running a command on the node: the start command, or
  the run command, run in a transient systemd service unit of the slice, as
  with `systemd_properties`. The check, info, stop, quiesce and resume
  commands run outside the unit, and are not filtered.
* A docker or podman container, and a task running one: the container, which
  the engine places in a scope of the slice with its systemd cgroup driver.
  With the cgroupfs driver the container is in no unit, and the resource
  refuses to start. A `--restart` option in `run_args` is refused too: om
  passes none and restarts the containers itself, the filter set first,
  where the engine would restart one unfiltered, at boot or when it exits.
  So is a `--cgroup-parent` or a `--cgroups` option in `run_args`, read
  after the placement of om: the engine would place the container out of
  the slice, unfiltered, which nothing verifies of a container run in the
  foreground, as the one of a task.
* A lxc container: the container and its monitor, which om starts in a
  transient systemd scope of the slice, `<slice>-lxc.scope`, lxc making its
  groups under the one of the scope. Every lxc container is started so, with
  rules or not, on a node running systemd on the unified cgroup hierarchy,
  so rules given later apply at once. The commands `lxc-attach` runs in the
  container join its group, and are filtered. A lxc taking no
  `lxc.cgroup.dir`, 2.1 or older, places the container out of the scope:
  with rules, the resource refuses to start.
* A kvm container: what the guest sends and what is sent to it, through
  the interfaces om makes for the networks of its `network` keyword. The
  filter is a nftables table of the `netdev` family by interface, named
  `osvc_netwarden_<interface>`: a pair of sets by rule, holding the
  addresses it resolves to, and two chains reading them in the order of the
  rules, a line by rule. It is loaded before the interface is plugged, and
  after the rules of `network_filter`, which keep the guest to its own
  addresses. Under the running guest, the addresses moving replace the
  elements of the sets, and a change of the list of rules replaces the
  table, each in one transaction. It needs `nft` and
  a kernel filtering what an interface sends to a guest, Linux 5.16 or
  later: the guest refuses to start where one is missing, and so does a
  guest with rules and no `network`, whose interfaces libvirt makes, as the
  ones of `bridge` or of a `virtinst` command. With rules, a guest
  exchanges arp, ipv4 and ipv6 frames only. What an interface needs to hold
  its address passes whatever the rules: arp, the ipv6 neighbour discovery,
  the dhcp exchange of the guest, and the icmp errors sent to it.

What is not:

* A command run by `om <path> exec` or entering a container, which is the
  operator's.
* A published port of a docker container served by its userland proxy: the
  packets reach the container from the gateway of its bridge, not from the
  client, so the filter sees the gateway. Allowing the gateway allows every
  client of the port.
* A vbox container, whose traffic the cgroup of its processes does not
  carry, and a kvm container with no `network`.
* What no socket of the processes filtered sends or receives. The filter of
  a cgroup judges the packets of the sockets of its processes, in whatever
  network namespace: what the kernel answers by itself in the network
  namespace of a container, as the reply to a ping, passes, and so does
  what that namespace forwards.
* A container whose root may configure its network. The filter keeps an
  application to its peers: it is no confinement of the root of a lxc
  container, or of a docker or podman container given `NET_ADMIN` or run
  privileged, who administers the network namespace the packets leave
  through, and is not to be relied on against it. A container to hold
  against its own root is run without that capability, in a user namespace,
  or as a kvm guest, whose filter is on the interfaces of the node.
